`@web/page` belongs to the Builder origin family with ExternalBinding backing.
See @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing) for the
package model. It requires an explicit `@web/page` dependency and isn't in the
implicit scope of `.mtf` files. It supports HTML-JS only. HTML-Wasm rejects
reachable calls.

### Browser snapshots

Every getter reads the browser on each call. These are synchronous snapshots,
not cached build values or subscriptions.

- `url() -> String`: full browser address
- `path() -> String`: location pathname
- `origin() -> String`: location origin
- `query() -> String`: location search, including `?` when present
- `fragment() -> String`: location hash, including `#` when present
- `title() -> String`: document title
- `viewport_width() -> I32`: viewport width in CSS pixels
- `viewport_height() -> I32`: viewport height in CSS pixels
- `pixel_ratio() -> F64`: device pixel ratio
- `scroll_x() -> F64`: horizontal scroll position
- `scroll_y() -> F64`: vertical scroll position
- `is_visible() -> Bool`: whether the document is visible
- `has_focus() -> Bool`: whether the document has focus
- `prefers_dark() -> Bool`: whether the browser prefers a dark colour scheme
- `prefers_reduced_motion() -> Bool`: whether the browser prefers reduced motion

`page.url()` reads the live browser address at runtime. For static links built
at compile time use @../site (@web/site)'s `site.url` instead.

### Navigation synchronisation

`sync_navigation(root_id String) -> Bool, Error!` updates active links and a
breadcrumb under the identified root. It requires these descendants:

- `data-page-home` on a home anchor supplying the first breadcrumb link
- `data-page-links` on a navigation element containing candidate anchors
- `data-page-current` on an initially empty breadcrumb container

It returns `true` for an exact link or home match and `false` for an unlisted
route. An empty root ID produces a typed invalid-argument error. A missing root
or required hook produces a typed resource-not-found error.

Matching uses same-origin paths, ignoring query and fragment. The helper marks
the active link and builds the breadcrumb from matching ancestors and the
current page. For an unlisted page it uses the first heading or document title.
It is idempotent, installs no listeners and doesn't navigate. Call it again
after History API changes.
